      "table": 2,
      "row": "COMAH",
      "column": "operability_assessment",
      "native_text": "Statutory instruments are the usual \nlegislative mechanism for amending \nCOMAH and have been relied on before (for \nexample, to implement the Seveso III \nDirective (Directive 2012/18/EU) in 2015). \nThis route can be used to remove the \nexclusion of temporary storage and add CO₂ \nas a named substance in Part 2 of Schedule \n1, providing the greatest levels of legal \ncertainty and clarity, but it will hinge on \npolicy developments. \nThe responsibility for reviewing the \nclassification of CO₂ under the CLP rests \nwith CCS stakeholders but could be \nfacilitated by the HSE through developing \nclear guidance on how CO₂ should be \nclassified in CCS contexts. This is a longer \nprocess and depends on greater \ncoordination between different \nstakeholders. \nTreating CO₂ as having equivalent major \naccident potential depends on the HSE’s \nreview of CCS hazards, drawing on new \nscientific and incident data and \ncollaboration with relevant stakeholders. \nThis route offers the advantage of not \nrequiring reclassification of CO₂ (which can \nhave implications for other regulations) or \ndirect amendment of COMAH, but it \ndepends on buy-in from regulated entities.",
      "native_lines": [
        "Statutory instruments are the usual ",
        "legislative mechanism for amending ",
        "COMAH and have been relied on before (for ",
        "example, to implement the Seveso III ",
        "Directive (Directive 2012/18/EU) in 2015). ",
        "This route can be used to remove the ",
        "exclusion of temporary storage and add CO₂ ",
        "as a named substance in Part 2 of Schedule ",
        "1, providing the greatest levels of legal ",
        "certainty and clarity, but it will hinge on ",
        "policy developments. ",
        "The responsibility for reviewing the ",
        "classification of CO₂ under the CLP rests ",
        "with CCS stakeholders but could be ",
        "facilitated by the HSE through developing ",
        "clear guidance on how CO₂ should be ",
        "classified in CCS contexts. This is a longer ",
        "process and depends on greater ",
        "coordination between different ",
        "stakeholders. ",
        "Treating CO₂ as having equivalent major ",
        "accident potential depends on the HSE’s ",
        "review of CCS hazards, drawing on new ",
        "scientific and incident data and ",
        "collaboration with relevant stakeholders. ",
        "This route offers the advantage of not ",
        "requiring reclassification of CO₂ (which can ",
        "have implications for other regulations) or ",
        "direct amendment of COMAH, but it ",
        "depends on buy-in from regulated entities."
      ],
